    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with **Trea Turner** (81.27) and **Kyle Schwarber** (79.18) acting as our offensive anchors. Both benefit immensely from a favorable encounter with Mor\u00e1n, whose 4.52 xERA and handedness alignment provide a distinct ceiling boost. **Turner** is in a prime position to capitalize on his leadoff spot, while **Schwarber** remains a high-floor play given his elite xwOBA of 0.399 and the park factor support. Similarly, **Junior Caminero** (70.52) and **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (69.07) are high-conviction starts; **Crow-Armstrong** specifically benefits from a +14% platoon adjustment against Holmes, making him a critical weapon for our stolen base deficit despite batting in the nine-hole.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision today involved **Chase Meidroth**, who finished just outside the starting nine with a score of 47.77. While **Meidroth** offers reliable production, the optimizer prioritized the specific situational advantages held by **Luke Raley** (60.89) against Imai. **Raley**\u2019s high SP skill leverage (+19%) against the 5.79 xERA of Imai ultimately provided more projectable value than the middle-infield alternatives. We are closely monitoring the status of **Casey Schmitt**, whose \"Lineup Pending\" designation and looming matchup against Yamamoto kept him on the bench; had he been confirmed in a more favorable order slot, he might have pushed **Colt Keith** (53.19) for a starting spot.\n\nRisks today are concentrated in our catching and late-order depth. **Hyeseong Kim** (56.83) is starting despite a minor efficiency penalty due to an elite opposing catcher, and **Christian Yelich** (55.69) faces a tough matchup against Rodriguez (3.34 xERA), which has suppressed his potential output. While we remain aggressive in chasing stolen bases, we have exercised caution with **Caleb Durbin** (39.67) and **Dillon Dingler** (37.92), as both fell below our 40.0 efficiency floor due to the elite arms they are facing. We are benching these players to preserve our limited game caps while trusting that the core of **Yordan Alvarez** (61.62) and **Kazuma Okamoto** (58.89) will provide enough middle-order stability to maintain our standing.",
